 ## Subtopics
 
-### Limited Secondary Ray Sampling
-A variant that optimizes the cost of self-shadowing by limiting the distance and number of samples for secondary rays [S2, unofficial].
-- **Step:** Use a linear scale for sample distribution along secondary rays [S2, unofficial]. Limit the maximum distance these rays travel to reduce computational cost [S2, unofficial]. Fade out the importance of samples further away along the secondary ray to avoid hard margins in the lighting calculation [S2, unofficial]. Terminate the primary ray sampling when the accumulated density reaches a threshold, treating the cloud as fully opaque [S2, unofficial].
-- **Parameter:** Secondary ray max distance is approximately 1000 meters, depending on scene size, limiting self-shadowing to a local area [S2, unofficial].
-- **Optimization:** Reduce the number of secondary samples for primary samples that are far away; this is effective with minimal quality impact [S2, unofficial]. Reduce the number of secondary samples for primary samples with low density, as the lighting accuracy is lower but acceptable because the sample is transparent [S2, unofficial].
+### Differentiable Self-Shadowing via Ray Marching
+This variant computes self-shadow visibility by sampling points along the direction from a surface point to the light to make the process differentiable for joint optimization of geometry and light [S2]. It calculates visibility $f_v(w_i; x)$ using the formula $f_v(w_i; x) = 1 - \sum_{j=1}^{N} \alpha_j \prod_{k=1}^{j-1} (1 - \alpha_k)$, where $\alpha_j$ is the discrete opacity at sample $j$ [S2]. This allows online computation for optimizing object geometry and light position simultaneously [S2].
+
+### [Limited Secondary Ray Sampling](6984-limited-secondary-ray-sampling.md)
+Limited Secondary Ray Sampling is an optimization strategy for computing volumetric self-shadowing in distance-field ray marching. It reduces the computational cost of secondary ray traces by limiting their maximum travel distance and adaptively reducing the number of samples based on primary ray depth and local density. Unlike global shadowing methods, it restricts self-shadowing to a local neighborhood, trading long-range accuracy for performance.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{\text{primary}} \cdot N_{\text{shadow}})$, where $N_{\text{primary}}$ is the number of samples along the view ray and $N_{\text{shadow}}$ is the number of shadow steps [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory per sample, assuming the density field is accessed via texture lookup [G].
-- **Performance:** Significantly more expensive than shadow map or transmittance function mapping methods [S1, unofficial]. On PC/Console, ~6 steps are typical [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Richer details than shadow maps due to lack of precision limits; no artifacts from shadow map resolution [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** High computational cost; prone to noise if steps are insufficient or too long [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When visual fidelity of self-shadowing is critical and performance budget allows for secondary ray marching [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When performance is constrained or when global shadows (clouds casting shadows on other objects) are required, in which case shadow mapping or transmittance function mapping may be preferred [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of shadow steps $N_s$. Since a secondary ray is marched for every primary sample, the cost is $O(N_p \cdot N_s)$, where $N_p$ is the number of primary samples.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per sample.
+- **Performance:** Significantly more expensive than shadow mapping or Transmittance Function Mapping (TFM). TFM with 7 Fourier coefficients is roughly equivalent to Ray Marching with 1-2 steps in efficiency [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** High detail, no shadow map aliasing, accurate volumetric attenuation [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** High computational cost, sensitive to step count (noise vs. brightness artifacts) [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When visual fidelity of self-shadowing is critical and hardware budget allows, or when shadow map precision is insufficient [S1, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When performance is paramount or for projecting shadows onto other objects (shadow mapping is preferred) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from techniques used in real-time volumetric cloud rendering, particularly in games like *Red Dead Redemption 2* and *Horizon Zero Dawn* which use ray marching for single scattering [S1, unofficial]. The specific approach of using ~6 steps for self-shadowing is documented in industry implementations [S1, unofficial]. Transmittance Function Mapping (TFM) and Beer's Shadow Map are alternative approaches discussed in the same context [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from real-time volumetric cloud rendering techniques, notably discussed in industry implementations such as those in Unreal Engine 4 and other high-fidelity cloud systems [S1, unofficial]. It is often contrasted with Beer's Shadow Map and Transmittance Function Mapping (TFM) in technical articles detailing cloud rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial].
 
